      "severity": "note",
      "claim": "The \"Notes for the agent\" section claims the edited CLAUDE.md:65 line \"still fits well under the file's own longest existing line.\" Measured: the new line would be 138 characters, while the longest existing line in CLAUDE.md is 141 characters. That is technically true but only a 3-character margin, not \"well under.\" This affects no acceptance criterion or witness and costs nothing to leave for the operator.",
      "criterion": null,
      "file": "CLAUDE.md",
      "line": 65,
      "fixes": null
    },
    {
      "severity": "concern",
      "claim": "The notes instruct the cell to \"Find the `saffron/task.py` layout bullet's line by its own leading text\" and then assert the shared qualifier appears in that found line. In the actual file, the bullet's leading text (\"- `saffron/task.py` — `run_task` drives one task end to end...\") sits on line 159, while the qualifier `whose cell raised but rebuilds green` is wrapped onto the bullet's third physical line, 161. A test written by literally splitting the file into lines and checking only the single line matched by the bullet's leading-text anchor would not find the qualifier there; it would need to search a multi-line window or the bullet's joined text instead. This is resolvable with a sensible implementation (e.g. checking the qualifier appears anywhere between the anchor and the next bullet, or checking file-wide containment given the substring's confirmed uniqueness), so the witness is not unmeasurable, but the \"leading text\" instruction is imprecise for this multi-line bullet and could mislead a literal implementation into a spurious failure.",
      "criterion": 1,
      "file": "CLAUDE.md",
      "line": 161,
      "fixes": "witness"
    }
  ]
}
